# Trivy image-vulnerability-scan exclusions — paired with
# .github/workflows/security.yml (trivy job) and .hadolint.yaml (same
# "document the exception, don't silence it blind" convention).
#
# Both entries below were investigated exhaustively, not waved through
# to unblock a pipeline — see each entry's own rationale.

# CVE-2025-47273 — reported as "setuptools 70.3.0" (fixed in 78.1.1).
#
# This does not correspond to anything actually present in the built
# image. requirements.txt pins setuptools==83.0.0 (well past the fixed
# version); `pip list`, `pip show`, and the build log all confirm
# 83.0.0 is the only setuptools pip itself knows about. A four-round
# diagnostic investigation (temporarily added to the Dockerfile,
# removed once concluded — see the v1.3.0 release PR history for the
# full trail) found:
#   - No file anywhere on the filesystem with "setuptools" in its path
#     outside the confirmed-clean setuptools-83.0.0 site-packages
#     install (unrestricted `find /`, no -xdev, no path filter).
#   - No dpkg-level python3-setuptools package (`dpkg -l` clean).
#   - No occurrence of the literal string "70.3.0" in any file under
#     site-packages/pip or site-packages/setuptools, nor in any
#     METADATA/PKG-INFO/egg-info anywhere in the image.
#   - `pip list` itself reports exactly one setuptools entry: 83.0.0.
# Conclusion: a Trivy scanner/DB matching artifact, not a real
# installed vulnerable copy. Re-investigate if this ever starts
# showing a different "installed version" than 70.3.0, or if a
# filesystem search someday does turn up a real match — that would
# mean the situation changed and this exception no longer applies.
CVE-2025-47273

# GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964 — msgpack 1.1.2 -> 1.2.1 (out-of-bounds read /
# crash on Unpacker reuse after an error).
#
# Real, but not ours to bump directly: this is pip's own internal
# vendored copy (site-packages/pip/_vendor/msgpack), used for pip's
# own HTTP cache serialization, not exposed to this app's own
# attacker-reachable input. pip 26.2.1 (what this image has) is the
# current latest release on PyPI as of when this was checked — no
# newer pip exists to bump to that might vendor a patched msgpack.
# Re-evaluate whenever pip is next bumped in this Dockerfile/CI
# tooling: if a newer pip vendors msgpack>=1.2.1, this entry can go.
GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964
